{"chunk_id": "3af0b35d5f6f", "text": "Book 9 (Shalya-parva) opens after Karna\u2019s death, when the Kaurava army collapses into despair. Duryodhana appoints Shalya as the final commander, but the remaining battle goes disastrously: Shalya is killed by Yudhishthira, the Kaurava forces are shattered, and Duryodhana eventually flees and is later slain by Bhima. In the aftermath, Sanjaya returns to Hastinapura and recounts the catastrophe to the blind king Dhritarashtra. The court is overwhelmed with grief as Dhritarashtra laments the destruction of his sons and allies and reflects on fate, his earlier warnings from Vidura, and the ruin brought by Duryodhana\u2019s choices. On the battlefield, Kripa advises Duryodhana to seek peace, arguing that Arjuna and Krishna cannot be defeated. Duryodhana rejects the counsel, insisting that honor demands continued battle despite certain ruin. The chapter highlights the themes of destiny, pride, and the tragic consequences of stubborn ambition.\nAfter Karna\u2019s death the Kaurava army collapses; Shalya is slain, Duryodhana is defeated and hides in a lake, and Dhritarashtra mourns the annihilation of his sons.\nThemes: aftermath of Karna's death, destiny and inevitability, royal lamentation, collapse of the Kaurava army, end of the war", "vector": [0.006827797740697861, -0.013469080440700054, -0.008906112983822823, 0.008479791693389416, -0.014041949063539505, 0.028270414099097252, -0.0223818551748991, -0.006754524074494839, -0.025885680690407753, 0.0019367631757631898, 0.02311459369957447, 0.007240796461701393, 0.008259969763457775, 0.02620542049407959, -0.003320641117170453, 0.0019667388405650854, 0.036024127155542374, -0.024020526558160782, 0.04196597635746002, -0.02952273190021515, -0.012749663554131985, -0.00837321113795042, -0.004439733922481537, 0.01985057257115841, -0.002771086758002639, 0.014255109243094921, 0.009145918302237988, -0.028376994654536247, 0.010071834549307823, -0.006887749303132296, -0.012936178594827652, -0